# Settings for `milestones`. Copy this to `.env` in the directory you run it from, or to
# ~/.config/milestones.env (beside milestones.toml), and uncomment what you need. A variable
# already set in your shell wins over both. `milestones doctor` shows what is in effect.

# The GitHub token every call is made with. Leave it unset to use `gh auth login`'s token,
# which can usually write code as well as issues. A fine-grained personal access token keeps
# this tool to what it should touch:
#   Repository permissions:    Issues: read and write; Pull requests: read and write;
#                              Metadata: read. Nothing for Contents.
#   Organization permissions:  Projects: read (to show what a project recorded).
# MILESTONES_GH_TOKEN=github_pat_...

# A fine-grained token belongs to one owner, so a second owner needs a second token. Name it
# after the owner, upper-cased, with anything that isn't a letter or digit as an underscore:
# NCATSTranslator -> NCATSTRANSLATOR, my-org -> MY_ORG. Calls about that owner's repos use
# it; everything else uses MILESTONES_GH_TOKEN, or gh's own login.
# MILESTONES_GH_TOKEN_NCATSTRANSLATOR=github_pat_...

# `read` makes every command say what it would change and change nothing: a way to see what
# milestones suggests before letting it loose. `write` is the default.
# MILESTONES_PERMISSIONS=read
